{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"evidence\":[{\"quote\":\"In promoting  a specific investment  product to a client, a licensed or \\nregistered  person should not offer any gift other than a discount of \\nfees or charges.\",\"source_path\":\"artifacts/interpretation/round13/current-code.pdf\"},{\"quote\":\"The underlying purpose of this requirement is to \\navoid the use of gifts to distract investors from the \\nfeatures and risks of a particular product.  In this \\nregard, if gifts are offered in such a way that they are \\nlinked to a particular type of investment product such \\nas certain types of funds or funds offered by certain \\nfund houses, then such gifts will be bound by this \\nrequirement as investors may be distracted from the \\nunique features and risks of such particular funds.\",\"source_path\":\"artifacts/interpretation/round14/sources-official/gifts-faq-original.pdf\"},{\"quote\":\"The underlying purpose of this requirement is to avoid the use of gifts to distract investors from the features and risks of a particular product. In this regard, if gifts are offered in such a way that they are linked to a particular type of investment product such as certain types of funds or funds offered by certain fund houses, then such gifts will be bound by this requirement as investors may be distracted from the unique features and risks of such particular funds.\",\"source_path\":\"artifacts/interpretation/round14/sources-official/gifts-faq.html\"}],\"missing_premises\":[\"No supplied selection states a typed component decomposition for a mixed package (for example, identifying investment-product consideration, fee discount, non-gift service, or other component types).\",\"No supplied selection states whether components must be analyzed separately when they are independently offered, separately priced, separately selectable, or separately documented.\",\"No supplied selection states an inseparability test or defines when a package is treated as one gift for paragraph 3.11.\",\"No supplied selection states a dominant-character, principal-purpose, or predominant-benefit test for mixed gift packages.\",\"No supplied selection explains how to classify a package containing both a permitted fee or charge discount and another benefit.\",\"No supplied selection provides adjudicated facts applying paragraph 3.11 to a mixed or bundled package.\",\"Applicability to a particular package, intermediary, client type, transaction structure, and timing remains unestablished from the supplied extracts.\"],\"next_source_questions\":[\"Does the SFC have official guidance, FAQ text, circulars, disciplinary decisions, tribunal or court decisions, or adjudicated interpretations that define component boundaries for a package containing multiple benefits offered in connection with an investment product?\",\"Has the SFC stated a separability test based on independent selection, pricing, documentation, delivery, or contractual obligation?\",\"Has the SFC stated an inseparability or dominant-character test, including the factors that determine the package's principal purpose or predominant benefit?\",\"How does paragraph 3.11 apply where one component is a discount of fees or charges and another component is a non-discount benefit?\",\"Are linked benefits treated as gifts when the investment product is only one component of a broader package, and what facts establish the required linkage?\",\"Is there an official SFC example or decision applying the purpose of avoiding distraction from product features and risks to a mixed package?\"],\"proposition\":\"The supplied SFC Code and FAQ support only that gifts offered while promoting a specific investment product are restricted (with a stated exception for discounts of fees or charges) and that gifts linked to a particular product type or fund house fall within the requirement because of the risk of distracting investors from product features and risks. They do not establish a typed component decomposition, separability or inseparability rule, or a dominant-character test for mixed gift packages. Any such test would be an inference requiring additional authoritative SFC guidance or adjudicated interpretation.\",\"question_id\":\"mixed-gifts\",\"status\":\"PARTIAL_SOURCE_SUPPORT\"}"}}
